{-
   The bit families - `Bitwise`, `Bounded`, `Signed`, the population counts, the field extractions
   and the permutations, with the fixed-width instances of `Bounded` beside them.

   No import: everything here is arithmetic on values the compiler already knows, and the machine
   instructions each one lowers to are attached by the compiler rather than named from source.
-}

{-
   Bit operations, over anything whose values have bits.

   The four are `&`, `|`, `^` and `~` under names that read: a type in this class can combine and
   complement its values, and says nothing about whether they are numbers. That distinction is the
   whole reason the class exists apart from `Integral`, whose superclass chain is `Num` and then
   `FromInt` - so an `OpenFlags` joining `Integral` to get four bitwise names would also have to say
   what `flags * flags` and the literal `3` mean, which is exactly what a newtype over a machine word
   is defending against. `Integral` has this as a *superclass* rather than repeating the four, so
   there is one declaration of `and` in the language and no call anywhere has to say which class it
   meant.

   The members are `Bool`, every integer width, a vector, a `Mask`, and any flags newtype that
   derives it. What is deliberately **not** here is `&&`, `||` and `!`: those are questions about
   truth rather than about bits, they answer `Bool` rather than the operand's type, and they are
   plain functions over `Truth` further down. See the note there for why that split is not a matter
   of taste - a `Logic` class holding both made `!flags` the complement of a flags value while
   `if flags` asked whether any bit was set, so the two disagreed about the same value.
-}
pub class Bitwise(a):
  fn and(lhs: a, rhs: a) -> a
  fn or(lhs: a, rhs: a) -> a
  fn xor(lhs: a, rhs: a) -> a
  fn not(value: a) -> a

pub class (Num(a), Bitwise(a)) Integral(a):
  fn rem(lhs: a, rhs: a) -> a
  fn %(lhs: a, rhs: a) -> a
  fn shl(lhs: a, rhs: a) -> a
  fn shr(lhs: a, rhs: a) -> a
  fn sar(lhs: a, rhs: a) -> a

  -- The two rotations, beside the three shifts and differing from them in the one way the name says:
  -- what leaves one end comes back at the other, so no bit of the value is lost at any distance.
  --
  -- **The count is taken modulo the type's width**, which is where they part company with the shifts
  -- above. `x \`shl\` 32` at a `U32` is whatever the target's shift does with a count its register
  -- cannot hold; `x \`rol\` 32` is `x`, on every target, because that is what the instruction does
  -- and what resolve/inst.def rules the operation to be.
  --
  -- Members of this class rather than of `Bits`, and at every width rather than at the two the
  -- machine has counting instructions for: a rotation *within* a `U8`'s eight bits is a pair of
  -- shifts the compiler can write out exactly, where a count *of* them is a question about a value
  -- whose register is four times as wide. `lowerCalc` writes that pair; at 32 and 64 bits, and over
  -- a vector, this is one instruction.
  fn rol(value: a, count: a) -> a
  fn ror(value: a, count: a) -> a


{-
   The two ends of a type's range - `Base.h`'s `maxLimit` / `minLimit` variable templates, said as a
   class.

   Two nullary members, selected by the type the *result* is asked to be, which is the same
   selection `zero() :: Vec(Int)` and `truncate(x) :: U8` already use. That is the whole reason this
   is a class and not a pair of `@platform`-selected constants: a generic body constrained on
   `Bounded(a)` can name the end of a range it has no way to write a literal for, which is what a
   parser wants at every overflow test and what `maximum`'s empty-container argument wants.

   **The floating instances answer the largest *finite* value**, not an infinity. Two reasons and
   both are about what a caller does with it: an accumulator seeded with `minValue()` is compared
   against, and a comparison against an infinity is fine but an *arithmetic* one is not - a caller
   that adds to the seed gets a NaN out of an infinity and a number out of `-FLT_MAX`. And there is
   no portable spelling of an infinity in this language yet, so a class member answering one would
   have to be an intrinsic where every other member here is a literal.

   Not instanced for `Bool` or `Ordering`. Both have ends, and neither has an `Ord` or an arithmetic
   for the ends to be useful *to* - the sole caller shape this class exists for is "seed an
   accumulator at the end of the range", and a two-valued type has `False` for that.
-}
pub class Bounded(a):
  fn minValue() -> a
  fn maxValue() -> a

{-
   A type whose values have a sign - `Base.h`'s `hasSign`, and the operation that fact is *for*.

   §2.5 of Analysis-Library.md asks for this as a marker class with `abs` beside it. It carries `abs`
   instead, for a reason that is not stylistic: an instance with no members has no source spelling -
   `instance Signed(Int)` wants a body it has nothing to put in - so a marker class is declarable and
   not instantiable. Carrying the one operation that needs a sign closes that and says the same
   thing, since a constraint `Signed(a)` is exactly as good a way to spell "this type has a sign".

   `abs` is a member rather than a function for `min`'s reason: `abs` over `Vec(a, n)` is already a
   plain function of Core, and R1 admits one of those per (name, arity).

   **The body is `<=` and not `<`**, which is the one trap in this class. `-0.0 < 0` is false, so a
   `<` would hand back the negative zero it was given; `0 - (-0.0)` is `+0.0` and `0 - (+0.0)` is
   also `+0.0`, so testing `<= 0` and negating is correct for both zeros. simd.cpp's vector `abs`
   makes the same choice for the same reason, and `1.0 / abs(0.0)` is the only portable way to see
   which zero came back.
-}
pub class (Num(a), Ord(a)) Signed(a):
  fn abs(value: a) -> a

-- Which way round the bytes of a value go, exchanged - `Mem/Memory.h`'s byte swaps, needed the
-- moment any binary format is read or written.
--
-- A class rather than one function per width, again because R1 admits one plain function per
-- (name, arity) and there are eight widths. A member of `Integral` would have been the other answer,
-- and the reason it is a class of its own is that a type can be `Integral` and have no byte order:
-- `WideInt` is 53 bits in a 64-bit register, so which eight bytes its are is a question with no
-- answer, and it is `Integral` all the same. A separate class is how a width says it has one.
--
-- The instances for the integer types are generated - one `ByteSwap` instruction each - rather than
-- eight bodies written out below the numeric instances. See the note there, and `defineByteSwap` in
-- resolve/intrinsic.cpp.
pub class (Integral(a)) ByteSwap(a):
  fn byteSwap(value: a) -> a

{-
   Which end of a word the low byte goes at, as a value.

   Two names and nothing else, which is what the two answers are. The numbering is pinned because
   the compiler agrees with it from the other side - `ReprTable::metric` answers `Target.byteOrder`
   with these numbers - so it is a fact this declaration is depended on for rather than one it
   happens to have, which is `Weekday`'s reason at a different scale.

   `Endian` and `ByteSwap` above are deliberately different words for what a reader might take to be
   one subject, and the split is the same one the class comment argues: a *width* either has bytes to
   reverse or does not, which is what joining `ByteSwap` says, while a *target* has an order, which
   is what this names. `WideInt` is the value of the distinction - it is `Integral`, it has no byte
   order of its own, and it runs on a machine that does.
-}
pub data Endian = @value(0) Little | @value(1) Big

{-
   **`Target.byteOrder` is a constant of this type**, and it is not declared here because it cannot
   be: its value is not one the resolver has. One resolution of this library serves every target, so
   a byte order is no more answerable at this stage than how wide a `Size` is - the read becomes a
   `byteorder` metric in the IR and whichever target is about to emit folds it to `Little` or `Big`.

   So it is supplied by the compiler, exactly as `Int` and `Size` are, and `addTargetConstant` in
   resolve/core.cpp is where it and its documentation live. What that buys a program is that
   `Target.byteOrder == Big` costs nothing at all and the arm it guards is gone before any machine
   sees it - which is the whole reason `bigEndian` below is written over it rather than as a
   `@platform` body per order.

   **On the JS target the answer is `Little`**, which is the order a `Uint32Array` overlaid on a
   `Uint8Array` reads on every machine a host runs on. A host array of bytes has no word order of its
   own, so what it reports there is the machine underneath rather than the language on top - and the
   word readers in `Container.yana` never ask, since `DataView` takes the order it wants as an
   argument.
-}

{-
   A value's bytes in a stated order, and the same function back again.

   **Each is its own inverse**, which is why there is no `toBig` beside `fromBig`: reversing bytes
   twice is the identity, so one name serves the read and the write, and two names for one function
   would only invite a caller to wonder which direction it is in. `bigEndian(v)` is "`v` with its
   bytes big-endian", read as a statement about the result whichever side of a transfer it is on.

   **On a target that already agrees, this is nothing at all** - not one instruction, and not a
   branch either. `Target.byteOrder` is the metric above, so the comparison folds where the target is
   known and the arm the answer does not take is gone before any machine sees it. That is the whole
   reason the constant exists: the alternative is a `@platform` body per order, which says the same
   thing in a place that cannot be checked against the target it names.
-}
pub fn (ByteSwap(a)) bigEndian(->value: a) -> a = match Target.byteOrder:
    Big -> value
    Little -> byteSwap(value)

pub fn (ByteSwap(a)) littleEndian(->value: a) -> a = match Target.byteOrder:
    Little -> value
    Big -> byteSwap(value)

{-
   Counting the bits of an integer - Tritium's `countBits`, `findFirstBit` and `findLastBit`, said
   once over a type variable and answered by an instruction on every target.

   A class rather than three plain functions for `ByteSwap`'s reason and one more of its own. R1
   admits one plain function per (name, arity) and there are six integer types with an answer here,
   so a `countBits` per width could never have been six functions; and a user's own newtype over a
   word is a plausible instance, which a class admits and a fixed set of overloads does not.

   **All three are total, and the answer at zero is stated rather than left to the machine.**
   `countBits(0)` is 0, and both zero counts answer the type's own width - 32 for a `U32`, 64 for a
   `U64`. That is the same ruling `/` and `%` take for a zero divisor: x86's bare `bsf` and `bsr`
   leave their destination *unwritten* at zero, which is not a value this language can name, so the
   alternative to a defined answer is undefined behaviour reachable from safe source. Every target
   has an instruction with the defined semantics at some level, and where one does not the compiler
   spends a conditional move rather than passing the gap on - see `expandBitScans` in
   codegen/x64/transform_expand.cpp.

   **Instanced at 32 and 64 bits and at no other width**, which is exactly the set Tritium declares
   these over and exactly the set the machine has. `U8` and `U16` widen first, and the widening is
   written where the caller can see it: a narrow value is held in a wider register here, so a count
   of its *storage* and a count of its value are different numbers and the conversion is the place
   that difference is settled. `WideInt` is 53 bits in a 64-bit register and is excluded for the
   reason `ByteSwap` excludes it - a leading-zero count is a question about a width it does not have.

   `trailingZeros` is Tritium's `findFirstBit` under the name the answer has rather than the one the
   search has, and `bitWidth(v) - 1` below is its `findLastBit`. Both of those are undefined at zero
   there and neither is here, which is the whole of the difference.
-}
pub class (Integral(a)) Bits(a):
  fn countBits(value: a) -> a
  fn leadingZeros(value: a) -> a
  fn trailingZeros(value: a) -> a

  -- The low `count` bits of a value, with everything above them cleared - x86's `bzhi`, and the
  -- scalar twin of `maskUpTo` below. What a tail wants: `n` elements are left, so `bitsUpTo(all, n)`
  -- is the mask over them, and nothing has to say what a bit index means twice.
  --
  -- **The count is read as an unsigned number and saturates at the type's width.** `bitsUpTo(x, 0)`
  -- is 0, `bitsUpTo(x, 32)` at a `U32` is `x`, and so is any larger count - so a caller that
  -- computed a count and did not bound it gets the answer the bound would have given rather than a
  -- number with a different meaning. That is the same choice `countBits`' zero case is: an operation
  -- reachable from safe source answers for every input it has.
  --
  -- **A member rather than a plain function**, for `bitWidth`'s reason at a different operation: the
  -- saturation is against the type's own width, and a body generic in `a` has no way to name it.
  -- An instance knows the width and the generated body is one instruction, which folds to an `and`
  -- against a literal wherever the count is a constant.
  fn bitsUpTo(value: a, count: a) -> a

  -- How many bits it takes to write the value - 0 for zero, 1 for one, 32 for a `U32` with its top
  -- bit set. Tritium's `findLastBit` is this minus one, and this is the form that has an answer at
  -- zero.
  --
  -- **A member rather than a function beside the class, because it is the one of the four that has
  -- to name the type's own width.** It is `width - leadingZeros(value)`, and a body generic in `a`
  -- has no way to write that width down: `leadingZeros(0)` would say it, except that a subexpression
  -- made only of literals resolves on its own and takes `default FromInt`, so the `0` is an `Int`
  -- and the subtraction has nothing that fits. An instance knows the width, so the instance is where
  -- it is written - and the generated one writes it as a constant, which folds the whole expression
  -- to nothing at a constant argument and to one subtraction at any other.
  fn bitWidth(value: a) -> a

{-
   `count` bits starting at `start`, moved down to bit 0 - the field extraction every packed binary
   format is read with, and x86's `bextr` said in the two numbers a caller has rather than in the one
   control word the encoding packs them into.

   **A plain function and not a member**, which is the difference from `bitsUpTo` above: this names no
   width of its own, so a body generic in `a` can write it, and R1 admits it because no other function
   of this name takes three arguments. A member would have been six generated bodies saying what one
   line says.

   **And not a `bextr` either, deliberately.** That instruction packs the start and the length into
   one register's two low bytes, so a caller with either of them in a variable pays an `or` and a
   shift to build the control word - and on Intel the instruction is two uops, against one each for
   the shift and the mask below. What this compiles to at v3 is `shrx` and `bzhi`, which is the
   sequence `bextr` is usually slower than; at a constant `start` and `count` it is one `shr` and one
   `and` against a literal.

   The two counts are named `start` and `count` because a call site can then say which is which:
   `bitfield(x, 8, 4)` reads ambiguously in either order and `bitfield(x, start: 8, count: 4)` does
   not. Nothing forces a caller to; the names are there so that the ones who want to, can.

   `start` at or above the width answers 0 for `bitsUpTo`'s reason read through the shift, and a
   `count` past the end of the value answers the value's own zeros - so there is no pair of arguments
   this refuses.
-}
pub fn (Bits(a), Integral(a)) bitfield(value: a, start: a, count: a) -> a = bitsUpTo(value `shr` start, count)

{-
   An arbitrary bit permutation, said as the two directions it has - x86's `pext` and `pdep`.

   `mask` selects positions. `gatherBits` takes the bits of `value` at those positions and packs them
   into a contiguous low field; `scatterBits` is its exact inverse, spreading a low field back out
   into them. So `scatterBits(gatherBits(v, m), m)` is `v and m`, which is the identity worth knowing
   about the pair.

   What they are for is the operations that are otherwise a loop: interleaving two numbers into a
   Morton code, reading a run of scattered flags as a small integer and writing it back, `select` and
   `rank` over a bitmap, and the packed formats a `bitfield` above cannot reach because their pieces
   are not adjacent.

   **A class of its own and not two members of `Bits`**, and the reason is what an instance is
   allowed to say. `Bits` is four counting operations that every target has an instruction for at
   some level; these are a *permutation*, which x86 has only at v3 and which most targets have never
   had. Keeping them apart is what lets a reader of a constraint know which of the two they are
   asking for, and it is `ByteSwap`'s argument at a different operation - a type can be `Integral` and
   have neither.

   **Both are total, and neither has a degenerate case to state**, which is the difference from every
   other class in this section: a zero mask gathers and scatters nothing, and a value with more bits
   than the mask has room for is answered by its own zeros. There is no count here for a width to
   saturate against.

   Instanced at 32 and 64 bits, which is `Bits`' set and for its reason. The instances are generated;
   see `defineBitPermute` in resolve/intrinsic.cpp, and the note in inst.def for what a target
   without the instruction spends instead - which is a loop on JS and a parallel-suffix network on an
   x86 below v3, and is the one operation in this file whose cost differs by more than a constant
   across the feature levels.
-}
pub class (Integral(a)) BitPermute(a):
  fn gatherBits(value: a, mask: a) -> a
  fn scatterBits(value: a, mask: a) -> a

{-
   The CRC-32C step - x86's `crc32`, and the one thing in this file that computes a named function
   rather than an operation on bits.

   `crc32(remainder, chunk)` folds `chunk` into the running remainder under the **Castagnoli**
   polynomial, reflected. That is the polynomial in the silicon and the class is not parameterized by
   one, because there is nothing a parameter could select: the IEEE polynomial that zip, gzip and PNG
   mean is a different function that this instruction cannot compute, and a caller who wants it wants
   the table in `Digest/Crc.yana`. The two live side by side there, and the note above them is where
   the difference is written down rather than here.

   **A class of its own and `@platform(x64)`, which is the only such attribute in this file.** Every
   other class here is something a target either has an instruction for or can spend a few operations
   on; a CRC is neither - a target without the instruction pays a two-kilobyte table and a dependent
   load per byte, which is not an expansion of anything and is not a thing the compiler should be
   writing on a caller's behalf. So on a target that has no `crc32` the class does not exist, a body
   naming it does not compile, and the library's own file selector is what routes such a build to the
   table. `defineCrc` in resolve/intrinsic.cpp asks the settings the same question this attribute
   asks, and the two must agree.

   **No feature level guards it.** SSE4.2 is inside x86-64-v2 and v2 is the backend's floor, so
   there is no x86-64 target here without the instruction - unlike `BitPermute` above, which is v3
   and has a network behind it below that level.

   Instanced at 32 and 64 bits, which is `Bits`' set and for its reason. The machine also has a form
   taking a single byte; the lower IR has no scalar below `Int32` for it to be told apart from the
   32-bit one, so a tail of fewer than four bytes reaches the table instead - which costs a handful
   of lookups once per message and nothing per block.

   The remainder is at the chunk's width rather than always thirty-two, which is what the machine
   does: the 64-bit form reads the remainder from the low half of its destination and writes a
   zero-extended answer, so the widen and the truncate a caller writes around it are what the
   instruction already performs and both fold away.
-}
@platform(x64) pub class (Integral(a)) Crc(a):
  fn crc32(remainder: a, chunk: a) -> a

-- Whether exactly one bit is set - `Math.h`'s `isPowerOf2`, and the test an allocator's size class
-- and `alignUp`'s undocumented precondition are both about. Zero has no bits set and is not one.
pub fn (Num(a), Integral(a), Eq(a)) isPowerOf2(value: a) -> Bool = value != 0 && (value `and` (value - 1)) == 0

-- The smallest power of two at or above the value, which is what a table doubling its capacity
-- wants. 1 for 0 and for 1, since there is no power of two below one, and **a value above the
-- largest power of two the type holds wraps to 0** rather than saturating - the shift is the
-- language's shift, and a saturating answer would be a comparison in the hot path of every growth.
pub fn (Bits(a), Num(a), Ord(a)) nextPowerOf2(value: a) -> a = if value <= 1 then 1 else 1 `shl` bitWidth(value - 1)

-- The value brought inside a pair of bounds. Written in terms of `min` and `max` rather than two
-- comparisons so that a type overriding either gets the behaviour it defined, and ordered so that a
-- `low` above `high` answers `high` - which is a documented answer rather than a checked error,
-- since the alternative is a bounds test in the hot path of every clamp.
pub fn (Ord(a)) clamp(->value: a, ->low: a, ->high: a) -> a = min(max(value, low), high)

-- Which side of zero a value is on, as a value of its own type. `0` for a zero of either sign and
-- for a NaN, which is the answer a caller multiplying by it wants: it is the only one that keeps
-- `signum(x) * abs(x)` from inventing a sign the input did not have.
pub fn (Signed(a)) signum(value: a) -> a = if value > 0 then 1 else if value < 0 then 0 - 1 else 0

-- Rounding up to a **power of two**, which is the form an allocator and a layout want: one add and
-- one mask, no division anywhere. `alignment` is required to be a power of two and nothing checks
-- it - a caller that passes 6 gets an answer that is not a multiple of 6 rather than a diagnostic,
-- on exactly the terms `@reclaimOnly` is trusted.
pub fn (Num(a), Integral(a)) alignUp(value: a, alignment: a) -> a = (value + alignment - 1) `and` not(alignment - 1)

-- The same intent for an arbitrary multiple, under a name that does not claim to be about
-- alignment. Two divisions' worth of work against the one above, which is why the two are separate
-- names rather than one function that tests its argument.
pub fn (Num(a), Integral(a)) roundUpTo(value: a, multiple: a) -> a = ((value + multiple - 1) / multiple) * multiple

{-
   The instances, one block per class, written here because the types are the compiler's own: an
   integer width is created before this source is read (`defineIntegerTypes`) and joins `Num`,
   `Ord` and `Integral` after it, so a body below may name any of them and a declaration may not.

   Every number in the `Bounded` block is a literal at the type's own width, except `U64`'s maximum:
   18446744073709551615 does not fit the signed `Long` a literal is read at, so it is written as the
   complement of zero - which is the same bits and is what every other unsigned maximum could have
   been written as.
-}
instance Bounded(I8):
    fn minValue() -> I8 = 0 - 127 - 1
    fn maxValue() -> I8 = 127

instance Bounded(I16):
    fn minValue() -> I16 = 0 - 32767 - 1
    fn maxValue() -> I16 = 32767

instance Bounded(I32):
    fn minValue() -> I32 = 0 - 2147483647 - 1
    fn maxValue() -> I32 = 2147483647

instance Bounded(I64):
    fn minValue() -> I64 = 0 - 9223372036854775807 - 1
    fn maxValue() -> I64 = 9223372036854775807

instance Bounded(Int):
    fn minValue() -> Int = 0 - 2147483647 - 1
    fn maxValue() -> Int = 2147483647

instance Bounded(Long):
    fn minValue() -> Long = 0 - 9223372036854775807 - 1
    fn maxValue() -> Long = 9223372036854775807

instance Bounded(WideInt):
    fn minValue() -> WideInt = 0 - 4503599627370495 - 1
    fn maxValue() -> WideInt = 4503599627370495

instance Bounded(U8):
    fn minValue() -> U8 = 0
    fn maxValue() -> U8 = 255

instance Bounded(U16):
    fn minValue() -> U16 = 0
    fn maxValue() -> U16 = 65535

instance Bounded(U32):
    fn minValue() -> U32 = 0
    fn maxValue() -> U32 = 4294967295

instance Bounded(U64):
    fn minValue() -> U64 = 0
    fn maxValue() -> U64 = not(0)

instance Bounded(Float):
    fn minValue() -> Float = 0.0 - 3.4028234663852886e38
    fn maxValue() -> Float = 3.4028234663852886e38

instance Bounded(Double):
    fn minValue() -> Double = 0.0 - 1.7976931348623157e308
    fn maxValue() -> Double = 1.7976931348623157e308

-- `abs` at every signed width. The unsigned types are deliberately absent: the absolute value of a
-- `U32` is the value, and an instance saying so would let a generic body constrained on `Signed(a)`
-- accept a type that has no negative to take the absolute value *of*.

instance Signed(I8):
    fn abs(value: I8) -> I8 = if value <= 0 then 0 - value else value

instance Signed(I16):
    fn abs(value: I16) -> I16 = if value <= 0 then 0 - value else value

instance Signed(I32):
    fn abs(value: I32) -> I32 = if value <= 0 then 0 - value else value

instance Signed(I64):
    fn abs(value: I64) -> I64 = if value <= 0 then 0 - value else value

instance Signed(Int):
    fn abs(value: Int) -> Int = if value <= 0 then 0 - value else value

instance Signed(Long):
    fn abs(value: Long) -> Long = if value <= 0 then 0 - value else value

instance Signed(WideInt):
    fn abs(value: WideInt) -> WideInt = if value <= 0 then 0 - value else value

instance Signed(Float):
    fn abs(value: Float) -> Float = if value <= 0.0 then 0.0 - value else value

instance Signed(Double):
    fn abs(value: Double) -> Double = if value <= 0.0 then 0.0 - value else value


-- The byte swaps are **generated**, one instruction each - `defineByteSwap` in resolve/intrinsic.cpp,
-- beside the `Num` and `Integral` instances of the same types and for the same reason. What they
-- would read like is what they read like until the compiler had an instruction for this:
--
--     instance ByteSwap(U16):
--         fn byteSwap(value: U16) -> U16 = ((value `shl` 8) `and` 65280) `or` ((value `shr` 8) `and` 255)
--
--     instance ByteSwap(U32):
--         fn byteSwap(value: U32) -> U32 =
--             (value `shl` 24) `or` ((value `and` 65280) `shl` 8) `or` ((value `shr` 8) `and` 65280) `or` (value `shr` 24)
--
--     instance ByteSwap(U64):
--         fn byteSwap(value: U64) -> U64:
--             let low = byteSwap(truncate(value) :: U32)
--             let high = byteSwap(truncate(value `shr` 32) :: U32)
--             return ((widen(low) :: U64) `shl` 32) `or` (widen(high) :: U64)
--
-- with each signed rung reading its unsigned partner through a `bitcast`, since `shr` on a value
-- whose top bit is set is the one place the two shifts disagree.
--
-- Eleven instructions at 64 bits, and one `bswap` on every machine this compiles for. The tree is
-- also what a backend would have had to *recognize* to reach that instruction, and a peephole
-- looking for a shape the optimizer may reassociate first is a peephole that stops finding it. So
-- the operation is a `ByteSwap` in the IR (resolve/inst.def rules on it), and these bodies are the
-- thing it replaced rather than the thing it is built out of.
--
-- The set is 16, 32 and 64 bits, which is what these instances covered: `U8` has nothing to reverse
-- and `WideInt` has 53 bits, which are not a whole number of bytes of anything.

-- What a condition means. `if x`, `if:` cases and `while x` ask this class rather than requiring
-- a Bool, so `if items:` and `if i - 1: continue` say what they look like they say. The rule that
-- keeps it from being JavaScript's truthiness is that it never applies through a conversion: the
-- instance is selected for the condition's own type, so what `if x` means depends on x's type
-- alone and not on which Widen instances happen to be in scope. Bool's instance is the identity.
--
-- Deliberately not instanced for Maybe or Result: they carry a payload, and `if maybeThing:`
-- invites unwrapping it on the next line. Truth answers "is this empty/zero/null", not "did this
-- succeed" - the `is` operator is what payload-carrying types use.
--
-- `value` is the default convention, which is already an immutable borrow: nothing about
-- truthiness needs to consume or write what it is asked about.
pub class Truth(a):
  fn truthy(value: a) -> Bool
